"""Fuchs data continuation, executed in the build_fragment helper namespace."""
Q='quillen1973higher';SEG='segal1968classifying';W='weibel2013kbook';MI='mishchenko1974infinite';AS4='atiyahsinger1971families'
TOP='Algebraic K-theory and categorical topology'
def ti(*args,**kwargs):
    kwargs.setdefault('subject',TOP)
    return item(*args,**kwargs)

talk(308,'1973-09-24','D. B. Fuchs','P000512-P000518','Quillen I: simplicial objects, classifying spaces and the Q-construction',r'The Fuchs heading starts on P000513 after the BDF discussion; this first installment ends on P000516. It gives a detailed introduction to simplicial sets and their realization, the nerve of a category and the classifying space of a group, before introducing exact categories and \(K_i(M)=\pi_{i+1}BQM\). The explicitly named source is \citet{quillen1973higher}, prepared in January--February 1973 after the Seattle 1972 conference. The scan faithfully contains two incorrect pedagogical sentences about realization, which are corrected separately from the valid construction.')
ti(308,'P000513-P000514','definition','Simplicial objects and the simplex category',r'A simplicial object is a contravariant functor from Ord, whose morphisms are nondecreasing maps of \(\{0,\ldots,p\}\) to \(\{0,\ldots,q\}\).',r'Let \(\Delta\) have objects \([n]=\{0,\ldots,n\}\), \(n\ge0\), and order-preserving maps. A simplicial object in a category \(\mathcal S\) is a functor \(\Delta^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathcal S\). The elementary injections omit one vertex; the elementary surjections identify adjacent vertices.',r'\citet[\S1, p.~105]{segal1968classifying}; \citet[\S1]{quillen1973higher}.',[SEG,Q])
ti(308,'P000514','claim','The face and degeneracy presentation',r'The two ladders have \(q+1\) face and \(q+1\) degeneracy arrows; the relations are said to be easy to write down.',r'The structure consists of \(d_i:S_n\to S_{n-1}\) and \(s_i:S_n\to S_{n+1}\), \(0\le i\le n\), subject to \(d_id_j=d_{j-1}d_i\) for \(i<j\), \(s_is_j=s_{j+1}s_i\) for \(i\le j\), and \(d_is_j=s_{j-1}d_i\) for \(i<j\), \(d_js_j=d_{j+1}s_j=1\), \(d_is_j=s_jd_{i-1}\) for \(i>j+1\).',r'This expands the functorial definition in \citet[\S1]{segal1968classifying}; no additional result is attributed to the speaker.',[SEG],reconstruction=r'The relations omitted by the notebook are supplied explicitly.')
ti(308,'P000514','construction','The singular simplicial set',r'\(S_q\) is the set of continuous maps \(\Delta^q\to X\); the face maps restrict and the degeneracy maps precompose with affine projections.',r'For a topological space \(X\), put \(\operatorname{Sing}(X)_q=C(\Delta^q,X)\), considered as a set. Precomposition with affine maps induced by \(\Delta\)-morphisms gives the simplicial structure. This is different from topologizing these function spaces as a simplicial space.',r'The construction is an instance of \citet[\S1]{segal1968classifying}; the distinction between sets and spaces is explicit there.',[SEG])
ti(308,'P000515','construction','Geometric realization and its CW cells',r'The notebook glues copies of \(\Delta^q\) for the nondegenerate simplices and calls the result a cell space.',r'For a simplicial set \(S\), \(|S|=(\coprod_{q\ge0}S_q\times\Delta^q)/\sim\), where \((S(\alpha)s,t)\sim(s,\alpha_*t)\). This is a CW complex with one open q-cell for each nondegenerate q-simplex. Attaching maps may identify boundary points and may map a face through a lower-dimensional degeneracy.',r'\citet[\S1, p.~105]{segal1968classifying}; \citet[\S1, realization preliminaries]{quillen1973higher}.',[SEG,Q],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The all-simplices quotient avoids the notebook\textquoteright s incorrect assumption that every face of a nondegenerate simplex stays nondegenerate.')
ti(308,'P000515','claim','Faces of nondegenerate simplices can be degenerate',r'The handwritten sentence says that arrows from right to left take nondegenerate simplices to nondegenerate simplices.',r'This is false for general simplicial sets. In the nerve of the group \(C_2=\{1,g\}\), the 2-simplex \((g,g)\) is nondegenerate, but its middle face is \(g^2=1\), the degenerate 1-simplex. Thus the page\textquoteright s own forthcoming group example supplies a counterexample.',r'The nerve and its degeneracies are defined in \citet[\S2--3]{segal1968classifying}. \ours\ The counterexample uses these definitions directly and was independently checked against the face and degeneracy formulas.',[SEG],literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction=r'The counterexample is editorial; the erroneous sentence is visibly in the scan and is not blamed on the transcription.')
ti(308,'P000515','claim','Realizing a simplicial space retains a topology',r'The note says a topological structure no longer arises for simplicial topological spaces, because the pieces have product topologies.',r'The same coend construction for a simplicial topological space has a quotient topology. What need not hold without additional conditions is the previous description as a CW complex with discrete cells indexed by simplices. If every level is a suitable compactly generated space, realization is taken in that category.',r'\citet[\S1, p.~106]{segal1968classifying} explicitly defines realization of simplicial spaces.',[SEG],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'Replacing ``topological structure'' by ``the preceding cell structure'' is a mathematical emendation, not a reading supplied by the manuscript.',development='annotated')
ti(308,'P000515','theorem','The singular realization comparison',r'For a good space, the realization of its singular complex is homotopy equivalent to the original space.',r'The evaluation map \(|\operatorname{Sing}X|\to X\), sending a simplex and barycentric point to its value, is a weak homotopy equivalence. If \(X\) has the homotopy type of a CW complex, it is a homotopy equivalence.',r'This is the standard realization--singular comparison underlying the classifying-space constructions in \citet[Chapter~IV, \S3]{weibel2013kbook}.',[W],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'``Good'' is replaced by the explicit CW-type condition. No homotopy-equivalence assertion is made for arbitrary spaces.',verification='indirect')
ti(308,'P000515','construction','An ordered simplicial model of a polyhedron',r'To reconstruct a polyhedron, the notes propose affine maps of simplices into it.',r'For an ordered simplicial complex \(K\), use the simplicial set whose q-simplices are weakly increasing lists of vertices lying in one simplex, allowing repetitions. Its realization is canonically homeomorphic to \(|K|\). Arbitrary affine maps with arbitrary vertex images are a larger construction and do not give this finite combinatorial description.',r'\citet[\S4, p.~108]{segal1968classifying} stresses the need to order vertices and the natural ordering of barycentric subdivision.',[SEG],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The vertex-preserving and order-preserving restrictions are supplied.')
ti(308,'P000515','theorem','Realization of products and the topology convention',r'\(\operatorname{real}(S\times S^{\prime})\to|S|\times|S^{\prime}|\) is asserted to be a homeomorphism.',r'For simplicial sets the natural map is a homeomorphism when the target product is formed in compactly generated spaces. It is also a homeomorphism with the ordinary product in the standard finite cases used here. The unqualified statement with arbitrary ordinary topological products needs this restriction.',r'\citet[\S1, p.~106]{segal1968classifying} and \citet[\S1, realization preliminaries]{quillen1973higher} state the k-space qualification.',[SEG,Q],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The notebook does not specify the category of spaces.')
ti(308,'P000515','construction','The simplicial interval',r'For the ordered segment, \(S_q\) has \(q+2\) elements, given by a switch from 0 to 1.',r'\(\Delta[1]_q=\operatorname{Hom}_{\Delta}([q],[1])\) consists of the q+2 weakly increasing binary strings. There are two nondegenerate 0-simplices and one nondegenerate 1-simplex, and no others. Its realization is the closed interval.',r'The interval-category construction in \citet[Proposition~2.1]{segal1968classifying} is exactly this model.',[SEG])
ti(308,'P000516','definition','The nerve and classifying space of a small category',r'\((NC)_q\) is the set of composable strings of q morphisms; faces discard endpoints or compose arrows, and degeneracies insert identities.',r'For a small category \(C\), \((NC)_q=\operatorname{Fun}([q],C)\) and \(BC=|NC|\). For \(0<i<q\), \(d_i\) composes the arrows adjacent to \(X_i\); \(d_0,d_q\) delete the first or last object; \(s_i\) repeats \(X_i\) with its identity arrow.',r'\citet[\S2]{segal1968classifying}; \citet[\S1]{quillen1973higher}.',[SEG,Q])
ti(308,'P000516','construction','Groups as one-object categories and the C2 example',r'A group is a one-object category, \((NG)_q=G^q\). For \(G=\mathbb Z_2\), there is one nondegenerate simplex in each dimension and the realization is \(\mathbb RP^{\infty}\).',r'For a discrete group \(G\), \(BG\) is a \(K(G,1)\). For \(C_2\), a string is nondegenerate exactly when none of its entries is the identity, so there is one open cell in each dimension. Its realization has the homotopy type of \(\mathbb RP^{\infty}\), the standard classifying space for \(C_2\).',r'\citet[\S3]{segal1968classifying}.',[SEG],reconstruction=r'The robust comparison is stated up to homotopy; the notebook does not construct a specific homeomorphism of CW models.')
ti(308,'P000513; P000516','construction','The first definition of the Q-construction and higher K-groups',r'An abelian category or an extension-closed full subcategory \(M\) gives a category \(QM\) of spans and \(K_i(M)=\pi_{i+1}(BQM)\).',r'Let \(M\) be an essentially small exact category. The objects of \(QM\) are those of \(M\); a morphism \(X\to Y\) is an isomorphism class of spans \(X\leftarrow E\to Y\) whose left map is an admissible epimorphism and right map an admissible monomorphism. Composition is by pullback. With basepoint the zero object, \(K_i(M)=\pi_{i+1}(BQM,0)\), \(i\ge0\).',r'\citet[\S2, pp.~99--103]{quillen1973higher}. The manuscript was prepared in early 1973; this is an exposition of it.',[Q],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'Admissibility, middle-object isomorphism and the basepoint are made explicit; record 311 gives the detailed occurrence.')
save()

talk(309,'1973-10-08','D. B. Fuchs','P000516-P000522','Categorical homotopy fibers and Mishchenko Fredholm representations',r'The continuation begins on P000517 (the page says following Marchenko, without a separate mathematical block attributable to Marchenko). Fuchs checks the square as a product of simplicial intervals, derives homotopies from natural transformations, and explains Quillen Theorem B using comma categories. P000519 explicitly changes to Mishchenko and continues through P000520: Fredholm representations, family-index pairings, and a K-class on a group classifying space. Because the catalogue supplies no separate Mishchenko record, those items are retained here with a speaker override. These are largely earlier constructions, not new unsolved questions.')
ti(309,'P000517','construction','The product of two simplicial intervals',r'The notes list the nondegenerate simplices of \(\Delta[1]\times\Delta[1]\), draw a diagonal square, and give a four-column array for the two triangles.',r'A q-simplex is a pair of increasing maps \([q]\to[1]\); it is degenerate exactly when both maps repeat the same adjacent pair. There are four nondegenerate vertices, five nondegenerate edges and two nondegenerate triangles, giving the usual triangulation of \([0,1]^2\). No simplex of larger dimension is nondegenerate.',r'This directly instantiates the product-realization construction of \citet[\S1]{segal1968classifying}.',[SEG],reconstruction=r'The four columns are read as two consecutive two-column triangles; no extra 2-simplices are inferred.',development='annotated')
ti(309,'P000517','definition','The nerve revisited',r'The definition \(BC=\operatorname{Real}NC\) is repeated, with objects, nonidentity arrows and commutative triangles as its first cells.',r'For a small category, the nondegenerate q-simplices are composable q-tuples of nonidentity arrows. Composite arrows occurring as faces may be identities; this does not alter nondegeneracy of the original tuple.',r'\citet[\S2]{segal1968classifying}; compare the correction in item 1973-308.5.',[SEG])
ti(309,'P000517','claim','Barycentric subdivision as a nerve',r'Barycentric subdivision of a polyhedron of high degree is said to be the nerve of a category.',r'For any simplicial complex \(K\), the poset of its nonempty simplices ordered by face inclusion is a small category; its nerve realizes to the barycentric subdivision of \(K\), hence to \(|K|\). No high-dimensional or repeated-subdivision hypothesis is needed.',r'\citet[\S4, p.~108]{segal1968classifying}.',[SEG],reconstruction=r'The face-poset category identifies the unnamed category; the unclear phrase about degree is not treated as a necessary mathematical condition.')
ti(309,'P000517-P000518','theorem','Natural transformations induce homotopies',r'\(B(C\times C^{\prime})=BC\times BC^{\prime}\). A natural transformation \(f\Rightarrow g\) is a functor \(C\times\{0\to1\}\to C^{\prime}\), hence a homotopy.',r'In compactly generated spaces, the classifying-space functor preserves finite products. A natural transformation determines \(BC\times[0,1]\to BC^{\prime}\), restricting to \(Bf\) and \(Bg\). For this interval product the usual topology already suffices.',r'\citet[Proposition~2.1 and following remark]{segal1968classifying}; \citet[\S1, Proposition~2]{quillen1973higher}.',[SEG,Q],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The category \(\{0\to1\}\) is distinguished from its realized interval.')
ti(309,'P000518','theorem','Initial and terminal objects give contractions',r'If \(C\) has an initial or terminal object, \(BC\) is contractible; it is called a cone.',r'A small category with an initial or terminal object has contractible classifying space. The unique arrows give a natural transformation between the identity and the corresponding constant functor, hence a contraction.',r'\citet[\S1, Corollary~2 to Proposition~2]{quillen1973higher}.',[Q],reconstruction=r'Contractibility is the invariant conclusion; a literal identification with a particular topological cone is unnecessary.')
ti(309,'P000518','theorem','Adjoints give homotopy equivalences',r'A functor with a left or a right adjoint induces a homotopy equivalence.',r'If \(F:C\to D\) has either adjoint \(G\), the unit and counit induce homotopies showing \(BF\) and \(BG\) to be homotopy inverses.',r'\citet[\S1, Corollary~1 to Proposition~2]{quillen1973higher}.',[Q])
ti(309,'P000518','theorem','Opposite categories have the same classifying space',r'\(BC^{\circ}=BC\).',r'For a small category, reversing each composable string and reversing its barycentric coordinates defines a canonical homeomorphism \(B(C^{\mathrm{op}})\cong BC\). This does not assert that the nerves are naturally isomorphic as simplicial sets.',r'\citet[\S1, opposite-category construction]{quillen1973higher}.',[Q],reconstruction=r'Equality is replaced by the actual canonical homeomorphism.')
ti(309,'P000518','theorem','The homotopy sequence of a fibration',r'The homotopy sequence of a Serre fibration is written, with an unmatched parenthesis in the fiber term.',r'For a pointed Serre fibration \(f:(X,x)\to(Y,y)\) with fiber \(F=f^{-1}(y)\), there is the exact sequence \(\cdots\to\pi_q(F,x)\to\pi_q(X,x)\to\pi_q(Y,y)\to\pi_{q-1}(F,x)\to\cdots\); in low degrees it is an exact sequence of pointed sets with the usual action interpretation.',r'This is the standard homotopy-fiber convention used by \citet[\S1, Theorem~B]{quillen1973higher}.',[Q],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'Basepoints and the low-degree qualification are supplied.')
ti(309,'P000518','construction','Mapping paths replace an arbitrary map by a fibration',r'\(\widetilde X=\{(x,s):s(0)=f(x)\}\), \(\widetilde f(x,s)=s(1)\), and \(\widetilde X\simeq X\).',r'For a continuous map between compactly generated spaces, give the path space the compact-open k-topology. The inclusion using constant paths \(X\to\widetilde X\) is a homotopy equivalence, and endpoint evaluation \(\widetilde f\) is a fibration. Its fiber at \(y\) is the homotopy fiber of \(f\).',r'The construction fixes the homotopy-fiber meaning in \citet[\S1, Theorem~B]{quillen1973higher}.',[Q],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The path-space topology is stated explicitly.')
ti(309,'P000518-P000519','construction','The comma-category map to the homotopy fiber',r'\(Y\backslash g\) has pairs \((X,u:Y\to gX)\). On a simplex, adjoining \(Y\) produces a larger simplex in \(BC^{\prime}\), and a straight path supplies a point of the homotopy fiber.',r'For a functor \(g:C\to D\) of small categories and \(Y\in D\), let \((Y\downarrow g)\) have objects \((X,u:Y\to gX)\) and arrows \(a:X\to X^{\prime}\) satisfying \(g(a)u=u^{\prime}\). Forgetting \(u\) gives a map to \(BC\), and the natural arrows from constant \(Y\) to \(gX\) give a canonical map \(B(Y\downarrow g)\to\operatorname{hofib}_Y(Bg)\).',r'\citet[\S1, pp.~93--97]{quillen1973higher}.',[Q],reconstruction=r'The notebook uses a reversed chain convention on P000519; the natural-transformation description makes the path compatible with faces and degeneracies.')
ti(309,'P000518-P000519','theorem','Quillen Theorem B',r'If every target arrow \(v:Y\to Y^{\prime}\) induces a homotopy equivalence \(B(Y^{\prime}\backslash g)\to B(Y\backslash g)\), the comma category models the homotopy fiber.',r'Assume for every morphism \(v:Y\to Y^{\prime}\) in \(D\) that precomposition \(v^*:(Y^{\prime}\downarrow g)\to(Y\downarrow g)\) induces a homotopy equivalence on classifying spaces. Then \(B(Y\downarrow g)\to\operatorname{hofib}_Y(Bg)\) is a homotopy equivalence for every \(Y\), and yields the corresponding homotopy exact sequence.',r'\citet[Theorem~B, p.~97]{quillen1973higher}.',[Q])
ti(309,'P000519','remark','A proposed limit over comma categories',r'Fuchs suggests that \(Y\backslash g\) should probably be defined as an inverse limit over all such categories with different \(Y\).',r'The notes do not specify an indexing category, the diagram, transition maps, ordinary versus homotopy limit, or the desired universal property. Theorem B already gives the precise assertion in the preceding item; this additional suggestion has no unique formal statement.',r'Comparison with \citet[\S1, Theorem~B and its proof]{quillen1973higher} does not identify an additional theorem with these quantifiers.',[Q],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',status='an exploratory comment, not a separate unresolved problem.')

# P000519 explicitly introduces Mishchenko; preserve that attribution inside record309.
def mi(*args,**kwargs):
    kwargs.setdefault('speaker','A. S. Mishchenko')
    kwargs.setdefault('subject','Operator algebras and index theory')
    return item(*args,**kwargs)
mi(309,'P000519','definition','Fredholm representations of a ring with involution',r'\(\Lambda\) has involution, \(T_1,T_2\) act on Hilbert spaces, \(F\) is Fredholm, and \(FT_1(\lambda)-T_2(\lambda)F\) is compact for every \(\lambda\).',r'For a unital *-ring \(\Lambda\), a Fredholm representation consists of unital *-representations \(T_i:\Lambda\to\mathcal B(H_i)\) on separable complex Hilbert spaces and a bounded Fredholm operator \(F:H_1\to H_2\), such that \(FT_1(\lambda)-T_2(\lambda)F\) is compact for every \(\lambda\in\Lambda\). For \(\Lambda=\mathbb Z[\pi]\), this is equivalently a pair of unitary group representations with a compact-intertwining Fredholm operator.',r'\citet[Definition~1.1]{mishchenko1974infinite}; the paper was submitted December 1972 and its central construction was announced in 1973. The notebook generalizes the definition from group rings to *-rings.',[MI,'mishchenko1973infinite'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'Boundedness, complex Hilbert spaces and preservation of involution are explicit.')
mi(309,'P000519-P000520','construction','Twisting a Fredholm cycle by a vector bundle',r'For \(\Lambda=C(M)\), the notes write \(F\otimes1_\eta\) on \(H_i\otimes_{C(M)}\Gamma(\eta)\), then take the index.',r'For compact \(M\), represent a vector bundle \(\eta\) by a projection \(e\in M_n(C(M))\). The compressed operator \(T_2(e)F^{(n)}T_1(e):T_1(e)H_1^n\to T_2(e)H_2^n\) is Fredholm. Its index defines an additive homomorphism \(K^0(M)\to\mathbb Z\). Because F intertwines only modulo compacts, the naive balanced tensor operator need not be literally well defined.',r'The Fredholm-cycle pairing is recalled in \citet[p.~702]{douglas1980extensions}; the family-index formalism is in \citet[\S2]{atiyahsinger1971families}.',[D78,AS4],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'Projection compression makes the shorthand tensor construction precise and avoids treating a compactly intertwining operator as an exact module homomorphism.',development='annotated')
mi(309,'P000520','claim','Integer-valued pairings lose torsion information',r'The first pairing is called an element of \(K_0(M)\), then corrected as too crude because \(K^0(M)\) can have torsion.',r'An integer-valued homomorphism annihilates torsion in \(K^0(M)\), and does not by itself specify every analytic K-homology class. The Fredholm cycle is the richer object; its numerical pairing is only one invariant.',r'The distinction between Fredholm cycles and their integer pairing is explicit in \citet[p.~702]{douglas1980extensions}.',[D78],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The initial identification is not retained as a definition of K-homology; it is qualified by the notebook itself.')
mi(309,'P000520','construction','Families refine the numerical index',r'For every \(Y\), \(T\) defines \(\operatorname{index}_T:K^0(M\times Y)\to K^0(Y)\), and this is said to be an element of \(K_0(M)\).',r'For compact finite CW complexes \(M,Y\), projection compression in a continuous family gives \(\operatorname{index}_T:K^0(M\times Y)\to K^0(Y)\), natural in Y. The Fredholm cycle defines an even analytic K-homology class, whose family pairings retain more structure than the numerical map at \(Y=\mathrm{pt}\).',r'\citet[\S2, family-index construction]{atiyahsinger1971families}; \citet[p.~702]{douglas1980extensions} gives the cycle interpretation.',[AS4,D78],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'Compact parameter spaces and the index-bundle construction are supplied. A full characterization of all natural transformations is not asserted.')
mi(309,'P000520','claim','Elliptic symbols and analytic K-homology',r'An elliptic operator has symbol in \(K^0(T^*M)\), compact commutators with functions, and K-homology class dual to its symbol.',r'For a closed smooth manifold M and a bounded order-zero elliptic pseudodifferential operator between complex vector bundles, commutators with multiplication by smooth functions are of negative order and compact on L2. It therefore determines an even Fredholm cycle. The symbol lies in compactly supported \(K_c^0(T^*M)\); under the symbol/analytic K-homology identification, its image is this cycle. A positive-order differential operator first requires a bounded transform or Sobolev normalization.',r'The symbol and family-index conventions are checked in \citet[Introduction and \S2]{atiyahsinger1971families}; the Fredholm-cycle framework is in \citet[p.~702]{douglas1980extensions}. The full duality identification is used here as the modern formulation of the notebook\textquoteright s Poincare-duality shorthand.',[AS4,D78],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='indirect',reconstruction=r'Compact supports, a closed manifold and bounded normalization are essential; the statement is not ordinary untwisted K-cohomological Poincare duality on an arbitrary manifold.')
mi(309,'P000519-P000520','construction','The K-class of a Fredholm representation of a discrete group',r'For \(\Lambda=\mathbb Z[\pi]\), a Fredholm representation determines \(\xi_T\in K^0(B\pi)\). The notebook gives the equivariant construction on the universal cover.',r'For a discrete group \(\pi\) and a CW model of \(B\pi\), choose on each orbit of vertices of \(E\pi\) the operator F and extend equivariantly by \(\widehat F(gx)=T_2(g)\widehat F(x)T_1(g)^{-1}\). All values differ by compacts; barycentric interpolation on simplices stays Fredholm and gives an equivariant norm-continuous family. It descends between Hilbert bundles over \(B\pi\). Trivializing them gives a class in representable \(K^0(B\pi)\), independent of the choices.',r'\citet[\S2, Lemma~2.1 and pp.~86--87]{mishchenko1974infinite}. The construction itself works beyond the finitely presented groups emphasized in that paper; the 1973 announcement predates this intervention.',[MI,'mishchenko1973infinite'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'For an infinite CW complex the K-group is representable K-theory. The stability of the Fredholm set under compact perturbations justifies each interpolation.')
mi(309,'P000520','claim','The Fredholm space has one component for each index',r'The notes use \(K(B\pi)=[B\pi,BU]\) and \(BU\simeq\mathcal F\), where \(\mathcal F\) is all Fredholm operators.',r'For infinite-dimensional separable complex H with the norm topology, \(\mathcal F(H)\simeq\mathbb Z\times BU\). The zero-index component alone has homotopy type BU. Consequently full K0 is represented by \(\mathbb Z\times BU\); reduced or fixed-index conventions must be stated to use BU alone.',r'The family-index description in \citet[Introduction and \S2]{atiyahsinger1971families} and the Fredholm-family use in \citet[\S2]{mishchenko1974infinite} require the index components.',[AS4,MI],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'The missing integer component is an emendation. A general F in the notebook is not assumed to have index zero.',development='annotated')
mi(309,'P000519','construction','Generalized signatures of Hermitian forms',r'For an arbitrary ring with involution and an invertible Hermitian matrix A, the notes announce an integer \(\operatorname{sign}_T A\).',r'Apply the two *-representations to an invertible Hermitian matrix \(A\in M_n(\Lambda)\). Their images are invertible self-adjoint operators; their positive and negative spectral subspaces are intertwined by \(F^{(n)}\) modulo compacts. The two diagonal compressions are Fredholm, and define \(\operatorname{sign}_T(A)=\operatorname{ind}F_+-\operatorname{ind}F_-\). For group rings this descends to the corresponding even Wall group in the stated signature degree.',r'\citet[\S3, Definition~3.4 and Theorem~3.5]{mishchenko1974infinite} supplies the construction and its group-ring invariance.',[MI],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The notebook gives no formula; the positive/negative spectral-compression formula is supplied from the paper. No analogous Wall-group descent for every unspecified *-ring is inferred.')
save()
